  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\n   The central idea is to use a dual-tree-based optimization approach combined with dimensionality reduction to efficiently find the function that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. Unlike local optimization methods, this approach leverages approximate nearest neighbor searches and parallelized gradient calculations to escape local optima while maintaining accuracy. The rationale is that by using adaptive dimensionality reduction and selective Pauli matrix vectorization for gradient computation, we can achieve faster convergence and lower C\u2085 values. Key limitations are the need for careful threshold selection and potential convergence issues in very high dimensions, but lower dimensions (around 114 variables) can mitigate these.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n   Representation: We represent the function h as x of length N, with entries in [0,1] and sum(x) = N/2.\n   Objective: Minimize\n        C\u2085 = (1 / (2*(N-1))) * max(|max(correlation(h, 1-h))|, |min(correlation(h, 1-h))|)\n   Constraints: 0 \u2264 x[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and sum(x) = N/2.\n   Algorithm: \n     - Phase 1: Dimensionality reduction by grouping points into K segments, retaining one variable per segment.\n     - Phase 2: Nearest neighbor graph construction using a dual-tree method.\n     - Phase 3: Gradient descent with Pauli basis vectorization for sparse-gradient updates.\n     - Phase 4: Check feasibility using the reduced problem's optimality conditions.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n   a. Input: N (number of points), pending_1300 (time budget), initial_h (if available)\n   b. Step 1: Initialize variables and check for initial_h.\n   c. Step 2: Dimensionality reduction:\n        K = ceil(sqrt(N))   (default)\n        Segment the points into K sections, compute means and project onto reduced space.\n   d. Step 3: Build sparse gradient representation using Pauli matrices for parallel updates.\n   e. Step 4: Solve reduced system with tree-based approximate  neighbors: \n        Use scipy's approximate nearest neighbors to identify relevant segments.\n   f. Step 5: Iterative optimization with projected gradient descent; check for improvement and stop if best C\u2085 is found.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n   a. Initial Point: Use initial_h if available, else uniform distribution.\n   b. Regularization Parameter (\u03bb): 0.1 (default) with range [0.01, 1]\n   c. Adam Optimizer with adaptive learning rate (0.01 default)\n   d. Reduce Top N: 10 (default) for neighbor selection\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n   a. Total Budget: 1300 seconds\n   b. Phase 1: Dimensionality reduction (time estimate: 0.5 * budget)\n   c. Phase 2: Optimizer runs (0.3 * budget) with adaptive time reduction on restart\n   d. Robustness: \n        - If NaN/Inf detected: Reduce learning rate\n        - If stuck in local minima: Perform restart with new initial point\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract:\n   a. Check candidate solution: Ensure sum(x) \u2248 N/2 and all x[i] in [0,1]\n   b. Re-correlate the final solution and return (x, c5_value, N)\n   c. If time constraint hit: Return best solution found\n   d. Final check: Project solution to ensure feasibility",
